the outer function changes faster there\",\"value\":\"larger\"},{\"label\":\"Because $f$ only ever receives $g(x)$ as its input, so its local rate of change matters at that input, not at $x$\",\"value\":\"input\"}], \"explanation\": \"$f$ never sees $x$. It only receives whatever $g$ outputs. When $x$ is nudged, $g(x)$ moves, and how strongly $f$ responds depends on $f$'s slope at the point where it is actually operating, which is $g(x)$. For example, $f'(x)$ would be the rate of $f$ near $x$, a place $f$ isn't even being used.\\n- The two rates are *not* at the same point. $g'$ is taken at $x$ and $f'$ at $g(x)$, because each function is used at a different input.\\n- \\\"Def"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":4,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":7627,"totalTokens":17093,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":7627},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860765269,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQiZniNb3FUWHSgBF2z"}}

